  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA shortwave passage will maintain scattered shower and \nthunderstorm activity for the next couple of days. Similar to \nyesterday, ML CAPE levels will be running in the 500 to 1,000 J/kg\nrange with effective shear generally between 25 and 35 kts. \nMinimum RHs will continue to dip into the 20s, so brief wind \nstrong wind gusts and cloud to ground lightning will be the \nprimary hazards; however, there will be hail and brief heavy \ndownpours with the stronger cores given the previously mentioned \nmodestly elevated shear and PWATs around 0.75 inches. \n\nRidging aloft strengthens over the Great Basin with heights \nrising as high as the 588 to 594 dm range over the Northern \nRockies by Friday. This will reduce shower and storm coverage while\ndiurnal mixing maintains seasonably breezy conditions. Despite the\nstrength of this ridging, weak surface fronts look to keep\ntemperatures mostly in the 80s over central and north-central MT.\nThese features will have a harder time surpassing the central/SW\nmountains, so upper 80s and even lower 90s are a safer bet down\nthere.\n\nSubtle shortwaves will begin to move into western/southwestern MT\nas early as Friday evening in advance of a trough moving onto the\nPacific NW coast. This wave will interact with diurnal \ninstability and initiate isolated shower and thunderstorm \nactivity, primarily over and near the higher terrain of W/SW MT \nwith localized gusty winds and lightning being the most logical \nhazards.\n\nThe Pacific NW trough then approaches the Northern Rockies during\nthe weekend and brings increased shower and thunderstorm activity\nand breezy to windy conditions. The main concern for this weekend\nlooks to be dry and continued very warm conditions and increasing\nfire weather concerns. Southwesterly flow aloft looks to develop \nin the wake of the exiting trough early next week with passing \nshortwaves maintaining daily scattered shower and thunderstorm \nactivity. - RCG \n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nMostly afternoon and evening showers and thunderstorms through \nTuesday night...\n\nScattered showers and thunderstorms will develop in a similar \nfashion as yesterday given the continued moist and modestly sheared \nenvironment. The one exception to this is the lack of a more defined \nforward moving surface front over the plains acting as a lifting \nmechanism. For this reason, storm coverage may be a bit more \nwidespread over the southwest, but the more isolated central/north-\ncentral MT storms are being highlighted with the better quality \nstorm maintenance and upscale growth, especially as they cross the \nhighway 87 corridor and the Little Belt Mountains. Should one of \nthese storms gain momentum, then localized severe wind gusts and \nlightning will be the primary hazards followed by brief downpours \nand hail. Other areas will see similar hazards to monitor, although \nwith less intensity in most cases given the mostly singular or weak \nmultiple cellular storm structure. Peak timing for today's showers \nand storms will generally fall between 2 and 9 pm with coverage \ndiminishing heading into the overnight hours. Tuesday looks to see a \nsimilar set up with scattered storms, mostly of the pulse variety \nwith few isolated stronger storms capable of localized strong, wind \ngusts, brief downpours, and hail in addition to cloud to ground \nlightning. \n\nElevated fire weather concerns this weekend...\n\nA Pacific trough looks to bring breezy to windy conditions to the \nNorthern Rockies this weekend despite increased shower and \nthunderstorm activity. Much of the forecast area has over a 50% \nchance for wind gusts exceeding 30 mph while minimum RHs fall to or \nnear critically low levels. This is also reflected in the Hot Dry & \nWindy Index (HDWI) with probabilities for exceeding the 75th \npercentile on Saturday running above 50% for most locations. Recent \nrainfall has tamed the previously maxed out Energy Release \nComponent (ERC) values and they are tentatively forecasted to \nremain shy of critical levels. If the mid-week warm up exceeds \nexpectations, then elevated to critical fire weather conditions may \ndevelop for some areas. This situation will continue to be monitored \nfor the need of fire weather products. - RCG",
